---
// A standing explainer teaching the DK (Diels–Kranz) fragments/testimonia
// taxonomy the fragment-presentation overhaul makes visible on the site —
// design note docs/freeman-wave-design.md §3.6. Copy is STATIC and shared
// across every dk-scheme author; only the chapter numeral/name and the
// citation-form grammar (noSeries) vary. Approved verbatim by John,
// 2026-07-23 (design note's post-draft rulings block).
//
// `variant`: 'full' renders the whole two-paragraph explainer (author
// landing page, between the lede and the Works list); 'condensed' renders
// one sentence pointing back to the author page (folded into a work
// reader page's own intro paragraph — ReaderShell.astro).
interface Props {
  authorName: string;
  dkChapter: number;
  noSeries?: boolean;
  variant?: 'full' | 'condensed';
  authorHref?: string; // required when variant is 'condensed'
}
const { authorName, dkChapter, noSeries = false, variant = 'full', authorHref } = Astro.props as Props;

// The design note's draft copy is written for the ordinary B-series
// citation grammar ("DK {N} B30"); a no_series work (Pythagoras, DK 14)
// swaps in its own "DK 14, 7" grammar per the design note's own
// instruction, rather than printing a fabricated "B30" that work never
// actually cites.
const citationExample = noSeries ? `DK ${dkChapter}, 7` : `DK ${dkChapter} B30`;
const jumpExample = noSeries ? '7' : 'B30';
---
{variant === 'full' ? (
  <div class="dk-explainer">
    <p>
      <strong>Fragments and testimonia.</strong> Almost nothing the Presocratics wrote
      survives as a whole book. Hermann Diels and Walther Kranz ("DK") gathered
      what remains into numbered chapters, one per thinker — {authorName} is chapter
      {' '}{dkChapter}. Each chapter holds two kinds of entry. <strong>A-numbers (testimonia)</strong> are
      ancient <em>reports about</em> the philosopher — what later writers such as
      Aristotle or Simplicius said he taught. <strong>B-numbers (fragments)</strong> are his
      <em> own words</em>, quoted verbatim by a later author. This site keeps the two as
      separate works.
    </p>
    <p>
      <strong>Reading a citation.</strong> <em>{citationExample}</em> means chapter {dkChapter}, fragment 30. A
      letter after the number (B8a, B14b) marks an entry Diels inserted between
      two others. The jump box takes any of "{jumpExample}", "{citationExample}", or "30". Each
      fragment also names, in smaller type, the ancient source that preserved it
      — e.g. "GALEN. in Hipp. de hum. I 1 [XVI 37 K.]" points to the passage of
      Galen doing the quoting. That source frame is apparatus, not the
      philosopher's own words.
    </p>
  </div>
) : (
  <span class="dk-explainer-condensed">
    {' '}Fragments are the philosopher's own words (B-numbers); testimonia are ancient
    reports about him (A-numbers) — see <a href={authorHref}>the {authorName} page</a> for how DK citations work on this site.
  </span>
)}

<style>
  .dk-explainer {
    font-family: var(--font-english);
    font-size: 0.92rem;
    line-height: 1.6;
    color: var(--text-light);
    background: var(--col-bg);
    border: 1px solid var(--border);
    border-radius: 6px;
    padding: 0.9rem 1.1rem;
    margin: 0 0 1.5rem;
  }
  .dk-explainer p { margin: 0 0 0.7rem; }
  .dk-explainer p:last-child { margin-bottom: 0; }
  .dk-explainer strong { color: var(--text); }
  .dk-explainer-condensed { color: var(--text-light); }
</style>
